## Proof of `erdos_942.variants.limsup`: $\limsup_{n\to\infty} h(n) = \infty$

We show: for every $k \geq 1$, there exist infinitely many $n$ with $h(n) \geq k+1$.

Fix $k$. Choose $k$ distinct squarefree integers $b_1, \ldots, b_k \geq 2$ (e.g., the first $k$ squarefree integers $\geq 2$). Set $\alpha_i = b_i^{3/2}$.

By the Corollary, $1/\alpha_1, \ldots, 1/\alpha_k$ are linearly independent over $\mathbb{Q}$. By the **multidimensional Kronecker theorem**, the set
$$\bigl\{\bigl(\{n/\alpha_1\}, \ldots, \{n/\alpha_k\}\bigr) : n \in \mathbb{N}\bigr\}$$
is dense in $[0,1)^k$.

Consider the open box $\mathcal{B} = \prod_{i=1}^k (1 - 1/\alpha_i,\; 1) \subset [0,1)^k$, which has positive measure. By density, infinitely many $n$ satisfy $\{n/\alpha_i\} \in (1 - 1/\alpha_i, 1)$ for all $i$.

For each such $n$ and each $i$, the interval $[n/\alpha_i, (n+1)/\alpha_i)$ contains a (unique) positive integer $a_i$, so $a_i^2 b_i^3 \in [n^2, (n+1)^2)$.

**Distinctness.** The powerful numbers $a_i^2 b_i^3$ have distinct squarefree parts $b_i$ (since the $b_i$ are distinct), so they are pairwise distinct. Moreover, $n^2 = n^2 \cdot 1^3$ has squarefree part $1$, so it is distinct from all $a_i^2 b_i^3$ (which have squarefree parts $b_i \geq 2$).

Therefore $h(n) \geq 1 + k$ for infinitely many $n$. Since $k$ is arbitrary:
$$\limsup_{n \to \infty} h(n) = \infty. \qquad \blacksquare$$

